This code snippet demonstrates a complete, compilable C program for the Raspberry Pi 3 Model B+ using the pigpio library to detect motion via an HC-SR501 PIR sensor connected to GPIO17 (physical pin 11). It initializes pigpio, configures GPIO17 as input with a pull-down resistor, and checks for initialization failures. The program polls the motion sensor every 100 ms, applying a 2-second debounce after each motion event. When motion is detected, it captures a timestamped JPEG snapshot using the Raspberry Pi Camera Module v2 and saves it to the "motion_captures" directory. The program takes a server IP and port as command-line arguments and sends a JSON payload containing the filename, an ISO 8601 timestamp with microseconds, the motion status, and the GPIO pin number to the specified HTTP endpoint using libcurl.

The code calls `curl_global_init` exactly once at startup and `curl_global_cleanup` at exit. It checks all system calls, libcurl operations, and file operations for errors, printing diagnostic messages to stderr. The program handles SIGINT and SIGTERM for clean shutdown, calling `gpioTerminate()` and `curl_global_cleanup()`.

